1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:08,070
This is a verification walkthrough of SUTRA, the platform submitted for the Gujarat Police CCTV Integration Challenge.

2
00:00:08,070 --> 00:00:15,964
Rather than a highlights reel, this runs the acceptance tests in the submitted test plan, one at a time, against the live system.

3
00:00:15,964 --> 00:00:22,160
Each check names the requirement it proves, and the result is shown on screen as it happens.

4
00:00:22,270 --> 00:00:28,198
Model One is the mandatory foundation: a central registry of every camera.

5
00:00:28,198 --> 00:00:38,524
The registry holds thirty eight cameras across seven departments, each with its location, district, protocol and live health — not placeholders.

6
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:40,872
Onboarding is automatic.

7
00:00:40,872 --> 00:00:50,913
Discover reads the challenge portal's own catalogue and updates all thirty government cameras in place, keeping their history rather than creating duplicates.

8
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:58,836
The registry is searchable and filterable by department, so an operator can narrow eighty thousand cameras to the ones they need.

9
00:00:58,940 --> 00:01:07,952
The whole registry exports to CSV — one row per camera with its full metadata, which is what a department needs for its own asset records.

10
00:01:08,060 --> 00:01:11,583
The Atlas map is the GIS half of Model One.

11
00:01:11,583 --> 00:01:22,466
It layers by department, camera type and status, with an optional coverage radius coloured by health, so a dead camera's zone reads as a gap rather than as cover.

12
00:01:22,580 --> 00:01:25,369
Camera health is reported honestly.

13
00:01:25,369 --> 00:01:33,005
Tiles read live, stalled, connecting, unreachable, queued or not pooled — and a tile that is not streaming says why.

14
00:01:33,005 --> 00:01:36,085
A frozen frame is never labelled live.

15
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:45,492
Gap analysis reports coverage by district, flagging thin coverage and ageing infrastructure — the planning output Model One exists to produce.

16
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:49,041
Every action against the registry is audited.

17
00:01:49,041 --> 00:01:55,767
The discovery and export just performed appear here, attributed to the signed-in user with a timestamp.

18
00:01:55,870 --> 00:01:59,271
Model Three federates heterogeneous sources.

19
00:01:59,271 --> 00:02:09,936
Government cameras arrive over authenticated R T S P while recorded file sources run alongside them, both reaching the same pipeline through one adapter contract.

20
00:02:10,050 --> 00:02:17,065
The portal authenticates every connection, and the stored source URL carries no email and no password.

21
00:02:17,065 --> 00:02:23,804
Credentials are injected at connection time and never written to the registry, the export, or the logs.

22
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:36,709
Detections are correlated against a government vehicle database through a VAHAN shaped connector — make, model, class, registering office and insurance status, with the owner name masked.

23
00:02:36,810 --> 00:02:41,529
Number plate recognition runs continuously on the government feeds.

24
00:02:41,529 --> 00:02:47,439
These reads carry the camera they came from, a timestamp, and the confidence of each read.

25
00:02:47,540 --> 00:02:52,177
The same vehicle read many times is one vehicle, not many rows.

26
00:02:52,177 --> 00:02:59,555
Raw reads collapse into sightings, and sightings into vehicles, so an operator sees vehicles rather than noise.

27
00:02:59,660 --> 00:03:05,398
Every read keeps the crop the recognition ran on, so any plate can be checked by a human.

28
00:03:05,398 --> 00:03:10,112
That is the minimum bar for anything supporting an investigation.

29
00:03:10,220 --> 00:03:13,227
Confidence is published with every read.

30
00:03:13,227 --> 00:03:17,343
These are plates fifty to ninety pixels tall on a live street.

31
00:03:17,343 --> 00:03:28,349
Reads above about zero point nine five are reliable; below that characters can be wrong, and the column lets a reader apply their own threshold instead of trusting all of them equally.

32
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:37,798
Beyond plates, every camera gets person and vehicle counts from an object detection sidecar — all on C P U, with no G P U anywhere in this deployment.

33
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:48,590
The watchlist holds stolen and suspect vehicles, each with an F I R reference and a priority, mapping one to one onto e-Guj-Cop records in production.

34
00:03:48,700 --> 00:03:55,416
Adding a vehicle takes effect immediately and is recorded in the audit trail against the user who added it.

35
00:03:55,520 --> 00:04:04,654
When a read matches the watchlist, an alert fires automatically carrying the camera, the location, the time, and an annotated evidence frame.

36
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:10,049
A watchlisted vehicle parked in one camera's view would otherwise fire endlessly.

37
00:04:10,049 --> 00:04:17,277
Alerts group into episodes — one row per vehicle per camera, with a hit count and how many remain unacknowledged.

38
00:04:17,379 --> 00:04:25,059
Where a match was fuzzy rather than exact, it is labelled a probable match and shows the characters the camera actually read.

39
00:04:25,059 --> 00:04:30,915
An operator is never handed a one character inference as a confirmed identification.

40
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:36,220
Alerts are acknowledged by an operator, and the acknowledgement is audited.

41
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:38,892
This is the evaluation scenario.

42
00:04:38,892 --> 00:04:45,631
Given a registration number, the platform returns that vehicle's history across the integrated network.

43
00:04:45,750 --> 00:04:56,868
The result is a timestamped, location-wise movement history: each sighting with its camera, district, time window, read confidence and evidence image.

44
00:04:56,980 --> 00:05:00,829
Sightings are plotted on the GIS map in sequence.

45
00:05:00,829 --> 00:05:12,527
Where a vehicle has been seen at one location only, the platform says so plainly rather than leaving an unexplained point — a route line is drawn as soon as a second camera reads the same plate.

46
00:05:12,630 --> 00:05:20,745
A registration number the network has never seen returns a clear not-sighted result, not an error and not an empty screen.

47
00:05:20,860 --> 00:05:33,106
The required output report exports directly from the platform: detected plates with their camera, location, confidence, and both U T C and I S T timestamps.

48
00:05:33,220 --> 00:05:36,021
Security is enforced server side.

49
00:05:36,021 --> 00:05:41,485
Requesting camera data without a session returns unauthorised rather than data.

50
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:45,055
Roles are enforced, not merely displayed.

51
00:05:45,055 --> 00:05:52,473
Signed in as a read-only viewer, the actions that change state are gone from the interface and refused by the server.

52
00:05:52,590 --> 00:05:57,458
That is the acceptance test plan run end to end against the live platform.

53
00:05:57,458 --> 00:06:03,694
The hosted instance, the source code, the output report and this test plan are all linked in the submission.
